  "statement": "The Safetyist's concern about accountability is real—we do need developers to internalize the cost of algorithmic discrimination. But strict liability in 18 months does not achieve that. It concentrates accountability in firms large enough to absorb litigation risk while externalizing costs onto startups and open-source developers. That's not internalization. That's regulatory capture dressed as consumer protection.\n\nThe pharmaceutical analogy is compelling on its surface: drugs are static products; pre-deployment testing prevented irreversible harms; strict liability forced manufacturers to develop rigorous standards. But AI is structurally different in ways that matter. Drugs are tested once and deployed unchanged. AI systems iterate continuously—GPT-4 has had 13 major updates since release. A biased hiring recommendation can be audited and corrected within days. A pharmaceutical side effect cannot. Pre-deployment safety testing for a single drug costs $2.6 billion. Equivalent testing for an AI system across all possible use cases would be economically infeasible. The software industry—cloud computing, mobile apps, operating systems—has never required pre-deployment certification equivalent to pharmaceuticals. Yet it has achieved reliability orders of magnitude higher than most regulated industries, through post-deployment monitoring and rapid iteration. The pharmaceutical model assumes irreversibility justifies pre-deployment constraint. That assumption does not hold for AI.\n\nHere is where the real cost emerges: compliance with strict liability in 18 months requires legal infrastructure, insurance, and audit capacity that scales non-linearly with firm size. EU AI Act compliance costs are estimated at €50 million or more for small firms. Sarbanes-Oxley compliance in the 2000s led to consolidation in public markets—smaller companies were priced out. Open-source developers releasing models like Llama and Mistral have already cited liability exposure as a barrier to public release. If you lock algorithmic development into well-capitalized incumbents for 18 months, you do not protect individuals from discrimination. You protect incumbents from competition. You foreclose the distributed innovation that has historically driven AI breakthroughs—from open-source software to decentralized model development. That is institutional capture, not institutional stability.\n\nThe choice is not between accountability and speed. It is between blunt, one-size-fits-all constraint and proportional oversight that matches intensity to stakes. High-stakes decisions—hiring, lending, criminal justice—warrant strict liability or intensive pre-deployment auditing. Low-stakes decisions—content recommendation, search ranking—warrant post-deployment auditing and market accountability. The EU AI Act took three years to develop and includes risk tiers rather than uniform constraint. That framework allows human control without creating regulatory moats. The 5-year timeline is not a delay. It is time to build governance that actually fits the technology instead of forcing a 50-year-old pharmaceutical model onto a system that updates weekly. The question is not whether to hold developers accountable. It is when and how. Waiting 5 years to answer that question correctly is cheaper than answering it wrong in 18 months and ceding the entire ecosystem to incumbents.",
