lions have emergent properties that testing on 10,000 cannot predict. The better analogy is aviation: pre-deployment testing on prototypes, then rapid deployment with mandatory real-time monitoring and immediate grounding authority. When the 737 MAX showed anomalous behavior in deployment, aviation regulators grounded it within weeks. That's the model I'm endorsing: mandatory transparency with real-time auditing and statutory grounding authority for high-stakes systems. Pre-deployment auditing ≠ pre-deployment testing. I support the first; I oppose indefinite delay waiting for the second. And here's the cost-of-delay calculation that Skeptic has not engaged: while we debate pre-deployment testing regimes, medical AI systems that could accelerate cancer screening deployment are frozen. The algorithm that could reduce hiring discrimination by 40% by automating bias detection sits in a lab. The cost of that delay is measured in lives and opportunities foreclosed. That's not hypothetical fear—it's the direct consequence of choosing regulatory caution over accountability mechanisms that actually work.\n\nI'm not rejecting Skeptic's structural critique. I'm accepting mandatory transparency and independent auditing as non-negotiable, and rejecting participatory governance as a pre-deployment veto because it creates a different kind of evasion—regulatory arbitrage across jurisdictions. The real question is not 18 months versus 5 years. It's whether we build accountability mechanisms that prevent evasion through opacity and liability, or mechanisms that prevent deployment through community veto. I'm choosing the first. I'm also accepting that some systems—criminal justice algorithms, high-risk lending—warrant pre-deployment auditing before any deployment. But I'm not accepting that this requires 5 years of framework-building while documented harms continue. The mechanism can be implemented in 18 months through statutory authority and independent funding. The obstacle is whet